speechtext:
Ask the minister; he will tell the hon. member better than I can. I have only a suspicion, but I think I know. I am not an authority; he is. Ask him. I have said enough on that to call attention to the situation. That is my duty and no more.
I assume as a matter of principle that when a bill of this sort is brought down by a member of the government, it ordinarily represents the considered policy of the government. I am disturbed to think that this is a bureaucrat's bill, as suggested, though not in those terms, by the hon. member for Vancouver South (Mr. Green). It is the settled policy of the government, for which of course it takes responsibility. Pension measures in days gone by have, of course, always been brought in in that way, but it was well understood that when they were referred to a special committee, as has been done as long as I can remember, the committee was not bound by the terms of government policy as expressed in the bill and was at liberty to make other recommendations. I was disturbed to hear the hon. member for Cape Breton South (Mr. Gillis) suggest that the committee would not be at liberty to propose, or at least that the government would not listen to, recommendations that might be brought in with regard to a change in the bill. I should 14873-82}
like the minister to reassure the house that this is not so, that the government will give heed to recommendations of the special committee. Having regard to the responsibility which the government has, as the custodian of the public purse, the treasury, at the present time-with that limitation-I think the minister might give consideration to the suggestion that I make, that when the special committee reports on matters of principle and matters of detail, the government will give favourable consideration as far as possible within certain limitations which I will not attempt to define at present.
